R7. Molecules flipping hand at random (racemization at rate r, units of k; g = 1e-3, eps = 1e-4)
      r predicted P simulated P final |ee|
    0.0       0.724       0.730      0.998
   0.05       0.715       0.731      0.946
    0.2       0.693       0.700      0.769
   0.45       0.667       0.654      0.073
    0.6         nan       0.580      0.018  (no choice made)
  -> flipping is symmetric: no new bias. It adds noise (P drifts toward 50%) and, if faster than the
     amplifier (2r > lam_max), stops the choice altogether. The weak-force tilt is the only bias.

R8. Beta-decay electrons as a second weak-force bias (g_beta = d*A/2, d = destruction rate / k)
  check (g_beta = g_pv = 1e-3, aligned): simulated P 0.889, predicted 0.883
  real scale: PVED g = 1e-17. With A = 3e-04, beta matches the PVED once d >= 7e-14,
  i.e. once about 1 molecule in 1e13 is destroyed by polarized electrons per reaction time.
  P(physics) for a borderline ocean (PVED alone gives Delta = 1, P = 0.84), beta aligned / opposed:
    d =   0e+00: g_beta = 0e+00; aligned P = 0.841, opposed P = 0.841
    d =   1e-14: g_beta = 1e-18; aligned P = 0.875, opposed P = 0.802
    d =   7e-14: g_beta = 1e-17; aligned P = 0.977, opposed P = 0.500
    d =   1e-12: g_beta = 1e-16; aligned P = 1.000, opposed P = 0.000
  -> if the lab asymmetry carried over to amino acids in water, even feeble radiolysis would rival the PVED,
     and the answer would hinge on the unknown sign of A. A for amino acids, at beta (keV-MeV) energies, in
     water, is unmeasured, so this stays a possible extra bias, not part of the main result.
